@maya 9:12 AM
Quick note from the facilities review yesterday: I think we should be more explicit in how we describe the cooling upgrades in the next customer update. A couple of people asked whether “containment” is just another marketing term, but it actually changes the airflow pattern in a pretty concrete way.

@dan 9:14 AM
Agreed. The simplest way to explain it is that hot-aisle and cold-aisle containment keep the air entering the servers separated from the air leaving them. That way, the cold supply air doesn’t mix as much with the hot exhaust air.

@maya 9:16 AM
Exactly. And that separation is what lets the cooling plant operate more efficiently. If we’re not constantly re-cooling air that already got warmed by the racks, the cooling systems can run at higher supply temperatures than they otherwise could.

@dan 9:19 AM
Right. That higher supply temp point is important because customers tend to assume “colder is always better,” which isn’t really true in a modern data center. Once the hot and cold streams are properly isolated, the system doesn’t need to deliver air at such low temperatures to maintain server inlet conditions.

@maya 9:23 AM
It also helps to frame it as an operational design choice rather than a one-off hardware feature. Containment is basically about directing the airflow more predictably: cold air goes to the front of the servers, hot exhaust goes away from the intake side, and the cooling equipment has a cleaner job.

@dan 9:27 AM
Yep. I can draft a short explanation that says something like: “Hot-aisle and cold-aisle containment separate the air entering servers from the air leaving them, reducing mixing and enabling higher supply temperatures for the cooling system.” That’s precise without getting too deep into thermodynamics.

@maya 9:31 AM
Sounds good. While we’re on facility metrics, we also need to be consistent about water usage language. There was a question from finance about how to compare sites that use evaporative cooling towers versus sites that don’t.

@dan 9:34 AM
That’s the one where WUE matters, right? Since evaporative cooling towers lose water as vapour, raw water draw alone can be misleading unless it’s normalized against the amount of computing work delivered.

@maya 9:38 AM
Yes. The metric we should use is water usage effectiveness, or WUE, typically reported in litres per kilowatt-hour. That gives a common basis for comparing data-centre water usage across different cooling designs and operating conditions.

@dan 9:41 AM
Makes sense. The vapour loss piece is the key detail people miss. In evaporative cooling, some of the water is intentionally turned into vapour as part of the heat rejection process, so you need a unit that reflects how much water is used per unit of IT energy.

@maya 9:45 AM
Exactly. If we just say “this site uses X gallons per day,” it doesn’t tell the full story. A large site running a lot of workloads will naturally have different water needs than a smaller site, even if the cooling technology is similar. WUE in litres per kilowatt-hour is a much cleaner way to describe efficiency.

@dan 9:49 AM
I’ll make sure the wording distinguishes the two topics clearly:
1) containment, which is about separating supply and exhaust air to improve cooling efficiency and allow higher supply temperatures; and
2) water reporting, where evaporative cooling towers are captured through WUE because water is lost as vapour and should be normalized as litres per kilowatt-hour.

@maya 9:53 AM
Perfect. Maybe also add a sentence that these metrics help us compare sites fairly. For example, one facility might have excellent airflow management because of containment, while another might have higher water intensity because its cooling strategy relies more heavily on evaporation. Different design choices, different trade-offs.

@dan 9:57 AM
Good call. I’ll keep it non-technical but accurate. Something like: “Containment improves airflow management, while WUE provides a standardized way to report water use for cooling systems that evaporate water as part of their operation.” That should land well with both technical and non-technical readers.

@maya 10:01 AM
Works for me. Also, if anyone asks why we mention supply temperature at all, we can point out that higher supply temperatures can reduce the amount of mechanical cooling needed, which is part of why containment is valuable. No need to overcomplicate it.

@dan 10:05 AM
Agreed. I’ll send a draft in the channel before noon. Thanks.
